{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>A man or a woman, when they clearly vow a vow of a nazirite, to consecrate themselves to HaShem.</b> Even though the Torah already equated woman with man for all punishments in the Torah, nevertheless it specified 'woman' here, because it is for this reason that the portion of the nazirite was juxtaposed to the portion of the sotah, to tell you that anyone who sees a sotah in her corruption should become a nazirite, abstaining from wine (Sotah 2a); and this applies more to women than to men, for presumably women would abstain from wine. And if you would say: it should have said ״כִּי יַפְלִיאוּ״ (when they vow), since it is speaking of both of them, in the manner stated (Numbers 5:6) ״אִישׁ אוֹ אִשָּׁה כִּי יַעֲשׂוּ״ and so forth — rather, because wine is more harmful to women, who are weak in strength, than to men, who are strong in strength, therefore if a woman abstains from wine it is not such a wonder, for it is easy for her to come to the pitfall of licentiousness, as our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Ketubot 65a): '[three, or] four, even a donkey would demand of her' and so forth; but the man — it is certainly a wonder if he abstains from wine, for he is stronger than it. Therefore it says ״כִּי יַפְלִא״ (when he vows)." "Another matter: ״כִּי יַפְלִא״ is an expression of distinction and separation. And this matter may be explained in three ways. The first is that through wine one cannot distinguish between sacred and profane, as written (Leviticus 10:9–10): ״יַיִן וְשֵׁכָר אַל תֵּשְׁתְּ״, and immediately adjacent to it: ״וּלְהַבְדִּיל בֵּין הַקֹּדֶשׁ לְחוֹל״. Therefore it says ״כִּי יַפְלִא״ — when one wishes to distinguish between the sacred and the profane, then one should make oneself a nazirite from wine, lest one drink and forget what was ordained (Proverbs 31:5) — the Torah — to exclude women, who are not obligated in Torah study, for the expression of הַפְלָאָה does not apply to them. The second is an expression of separation from the pleasures of this world, of which wine is the head of them all, as written that for this reason it was said to Manoah: ״לָמָּה זֶּה תִּשְׁאַל לִשְׁמִי וְהוּא פֶלִאי״ (Judges 13:18) — that is, 'Peli' is his name, for every angel is called by the name of its mission, and I have been sent to make the child to be born a nazirite who is מֻפְלָא and מוּפְרָשׁ (distinct and separated) from all the matters of this world. And by the name of which it says ״אִישׁ כִּי יַפְלִא״, therefore I am called Peli. And therefore it says ״כִּי יַפְלִא״ and not ״יַפְלִיאוּ״, because also a woman's vow depends on her husband, for after all ״כָּל נֶדֶר לְעַנּוֹת נָפֶשׁ אִישָׁהּ יְקִימֶנּוּ וְאִישָׁהּ יְפֵרֶנּוּ״ (Numbers 30:14) — if so, her vow too depends on him. And the intention in this naziriteship is not that one should be separated from wine for thirty days and then return to being a wine-drinker as before, for if so, what did the righteous person accomplish? Rather, the intention is that one should accustom oneself little by little, for all beginnings are hard, and in thirty days it becomes an established fact that one will no longer be so inflamed after it. The third is, along the lines of what our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Nedarim 22b): one who vows is as if one built a bama (forbidden altar) and so forth — and this statement you will find a very precious explanation for further on, in the portion of Matot (Numbers 30:3); see there. And this accords with the one who says that the nazirite is called a sinner (Nedarim 10a), and the sin is that one builds a bama for oneself and separates oneself from the entire world to practice prohibition in a matter that everyone practices as permitted. Therefore it says ״כִּי יַפְלִא״ — that is, one wishes to be distinguished and separated from one's fellows, building a bama for oneself. And this matter is not found among women, for presumably their intent is because they see the sotah in her corruption, and not in order to build a bama for themselves."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The bracketed text [(שְׁלֹשָׁה) אַרְבָּעָה] reflects a variant reading in the Talmudic citation (Ketubot 65a), where some texts read 'three' and others 'four'; the brackets indicate the correction in standard editions."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:39:58.384547119Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}